Findings
The “reading of text messages” and “texting” had a big impact on the “change of the steering position per second. For all three cell phone assignments, a substantial main effect was seen in terms of “following distance per second” and “change of the lateral lane position per second”.
The disruptive variables have a negative impact on road safety due to cognitive distraction and mobility limitation (e.g., longer response times and more errors), on the one hand, and have a bad impact on the environment and the economy (e.g., increased fuel consumption), on the other.
The ﬁndings indicated that new features with the greatest levels of urgency and criticality, such as Emergency Vehicle Warning (EVW) and Emergency Electronic Brake Lights (EEBL), would improve safety and make it easier for emergency vehicles to reach their intervention site.
By using a phone instead of just driving, participants shown increased off-road visual attention.
The time spent on tasks was marginally longer for participants who anticipated dangers compared to those who did not, but the difference was stable across tasks.
When drivers were texting, the perceived workload increased, but balancing training decreased it. While seated on the unsteady surface, perceived workload was higher; however, it decreased after balance training.
Glass-delivered messages served to reduce distracting cognitive demands, but they did not completely remove them. Comparatively speaking to driving when not multitasking, messaging while using either gadget impairs driving.
Web browsing and texting-related distraction raise the likelihood of an accident, the headway, and the lateral distance deviation by 32%, 27%, and 6%, respectively.
There was no difference in the number of vehicles overtaken between the groups, and the existence of the speed warnings had no effect on overtaking.
Compared to the no task and the cell-phone task, the lane position varied signiﬁcantly more while texting. Teens with ADHD spent noticeably less time to ﬁnish the scenario while texting in particular. There were no discernible group-wide major effects detected.

